                %li
                  %a{:href => "#in_terms_of_adjoint_pairs_of_modal_operators"} In terms of adjoint pairs of modal operators
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#examples_2"} Examples
          %h2#idea Idea
          %div{:style => "float:right;margin:10px 10px 10px 10px;"}
            %img{:src => "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Cup_or_faces_paradox.svg/450px-Cup_or_faces_paradox.svg.png", :width => "200"}/
          %p
            The concept of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction"} adjunction
            as such expresses a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality"} duality
            %em adjoint cylinder
            or
            %em
              adjoint
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modality"} modality
            is specifically an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction"} adjunction
            between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/idempotent+monad"} idempotent (co-)monads
            and is meant to express specifically a
            = succeed "." do
              %em duality between opposites
          %p
            In terms of the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+triple"} adjoint triple
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflective+subcategory"} (co-)reflections
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localizations"} localizations
            the concept was suggested in (
            %a{:href => "#Lawvere91"}> Lawvere 91, p. 7
            ,
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Lawvere94"} Lawvere 94, p. 11
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Georg+Hegel"} Georg Hegel
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Science+of+Logic"} Science of Logic
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/paradox"} paradox
            %a{:href => "#Wikipedia"} this Wikipeda entry
            ).
          %p
            In terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+pairs"} adjoint pairs
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+operators"} modal operators
            in the context of
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+logic"} modal logic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+type+theory"} modal type theory
            and thought of as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Galois+connections"} Galois connections
            the concept appears in (
            %a{:href => "#ReyesZolfaghari91"}> Reyes-Zolfaghari 91
            ). Further developments along these lines include (
            %a{:href => "#DJK14"}> DJK 14
            ).
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            In (
            %a{:href => "#Lawvere94"}> Lawvere 94
            ) an
            %em adjoint cylinder
            is defined to be an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+triple"} adjoint triple
            such that the outer two adjoints are
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+and+faithful+functors"} full and faithful functors
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+pair"} adjoint pair
            on the codomain of these inclusions consists of an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/idempotent+monad"} idempotent
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad"} monad
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comonad"} comonad
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+monads"}> adjoint monads
            ). One may also consider the situation where the middle functor of the adjoint triple is fully faithful, hence one has adjoint
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+operators"} modal operators
            either of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi modality
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mi comodality
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  U \;\colon\; modality \dashv comodality
                  \\,,
          %p or of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi comodality
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mi modality
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  U \;\colon\; comodality \dashv modality
                  \\,.
          %p
            A category equipped with an adjoint modality of the second form is called a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+being"} category of being
            in (
            %a{:href => "#Lawvere91"}> Lawvere 91
            ). If the category is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            then this is also called a
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/level+of+a+topos"} level of a topos
          %p
            Given any such, we may say that the “unity” expressed by the two opposites is exhibited by the canonical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformation"} natural transformation
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mi X
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi comodal
                          %mi X
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⟶
                        %mtd
                          %mi X
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⟶
                        %mtd
                          %mi modal
                          %mi X
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi opposite
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mn 1
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi unity
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi opposite
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  U X
                  \\;\colon\;
                  \\array{
                  comodal X &amp;\longrightarrow&amp; X &amp;\longrightarrow&amp; modal X
                  \\\
                  opposite\;1 &amp;&amp; unity &amp;&amp; opposite\;2
                  }
          %p
            which is the composite of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/counit+of+a+comonad"} counit of the comodality
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit+of+a+monad"} unit of the modality
            (
            %a{:href => "#LawvereRosebrugh03"}> Lawvere-Rosebrugh 03, p. 245
            ).
          %p
            If for two
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/level+of+a+topos"} levels
            the next one contains the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+types"} modal types
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/idempotent+comonad"} idempotent comonad
            of the former, then
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere"} Lawvere
            speaks of “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Aufhebung"}> Aufhebung
            ” (see there for more).
          %p
            One can consider longer sequences of such adjoints of co/modalities, but the longer they get, the less likely they are to be non-trivial. The longest that still has good nontrivial models seems to be
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+triples"} adjoint triples
            of modalities. Of these there is then similarly either the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi modality
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mi comodality
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mi modality
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  modality \dashv comodality \dashv modality
          %p
            (the “Yin triple”) as for instance in the definition of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesion"} cohesion
            and
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi comodality
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mi modality
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mi comodality
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  comodality \dashv modality \dashv comodality
          %p
            (the “Yang triple”) as for instance in the definition of
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohesion"} differential cohesion
          %p
            Since adjoint triples are equivalently
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunctions"} adjunctions
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunctions"} adjunctions
            (
            %a{:href => "#LicataShulman"}> Licata-Shulman, section 5
            ), it is suggestive to denote these as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo ◊
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⊣
                        %mtd
                          %mo ◯
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⊥
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⊥
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo ◯
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⊣
                        %mtd
                          %mo □
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \\lozenge &amp;\dashv&amp; \bigcirc
                  \\\
                  \\bot &amp;&amp; \bot
                  \\\
                  \\bigcirc &amp;\dashv&amp; \Box
                  }
          %h2#examples Examples
          %h3#SimpleIllustrativeExamples Simple illustrative examples
          %p
            The following simple illustrative example of an adjunction of the form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo □
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mo ◯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Box \dashv \bigcirc
            has been suggested in (
            %a{:href => "#Lawvere2000"}> Lawvere 00
            ).
          #EvenAndOddIntegersAdjointModality.num_example
            %h6#example Example
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/even+number"} even
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/even+number"} odd
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integers"} integers
            %p
              Regard the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integers"} integers
              as a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/preordered+set"} preordered set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ≤
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathbb{Z}, \leq)
              in the canonical way, and thus as a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/thin+category"} thin category
            %p
              Consider the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+subcategory"} full subcategory
              inclusions
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi ℤ
                            %mo ,
                            %mo ≤
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo ↪
                              %mi even
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi ℤ
                            %mo ,
                            %mo ≤
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi n
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↦
                          %mtd
                            %mn 2
                            %mi n
                    %mphantom
                      %mi AAAAA
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi ℤ
                            %mo ,
                            %mo ≤
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo ↪
                              %mi odd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi ℤ
                            %mo ,
                            %mo ≤
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi n
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↦
                          %mtd
                            %mn 2
                            %mi n
                            %mo +
                            %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    (\mathbb{Z}, \leq )
                    \&amp; \overset{even}{\hookrightarrow}&amp;
                    (\mathbb{Z},\leq)
                    \\\
                    n &amp;\mapsto &amp; 2 n
                    }
                    \\phantom{AAAAA}
                    \\array{
                    (\mathbb{Z}, \leq )
                    \&amp; \overset{odd}{\hookrightarrow}&amp;
                    (\mathbb{Z},\leq)
                    \\\
                    n &amp;\mapsto &amp; 2 n + 1
                    }
            %p
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/even+number"} even
              and the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/even+number"} odd
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integers"} integers
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi ℤ
                            %mo ,
                            %mo ≤
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo ⟶
                              %mrow
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⌊
                                %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                                %mn 2
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⌋
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi ℤ
                            %mo ,
                            %mo ≤
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi n
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↦
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⌊
                            %mi n
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                            %mn 2
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⌋
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    (\mathbb{Z}, \leq )
                    \&amp; \overset{\lfloor-/2\rfloor}{\longrightarrow}&amp;
                    (\mathbb{Z},\leq)
                    \\\
                    n &amp;\mapsto&amp; \lfloor n/2 \rfloor
                    }
            %p
              which sends any
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              to the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/floor"} floor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⌊
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⌋
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lfloor n/2 \rfloor
              of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n/2
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+number"} rational number
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n/2
            %p
              These functors form an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+triple"} adjoint triple
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi even
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⌊
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⌋
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi odd
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    even \;\dashv\; \lfloor -/2 \rfloor \;\dashv\; odd
            %p and hence induce an adjoint modality
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Even
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi Odd
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    Even \;\dashv\; Odd
            %p
              on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ≤
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathbb{Z}, \leq)
              with
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Even
                        %mo ≔
                        %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⌊
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⌋
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Even \coloneqq 2 \lfloor -/2 \rfloor
                  sending any integer to its “even
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/floor"} floor
                  value”
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Odd
                        %mo ≔
                        %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⌊
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⌋
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Odd \coloneqq 2 \lfloor -/2 \rfloor + 1
                  sending any integer to its “odd
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ceiling"} ceiling
                  value”.
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              Observe that for all
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℤ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in \mathbb{Z}
              we have
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⌊
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⌋
                    %mover
                      %mo ≤
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi ϵ
                          %mi n
                    %mi n
                    %mover
                      %mo ≤
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi η
                          %mi n
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⌊
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⌋
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    2 \lfloor n/2 \rfloor
                    \\overset{ \epsilon_n }{\leq}
                    n
                    \\overset{ \eta_n }{\leq}
                    2 \lfloor n/2 \rfloor  + 1
                    \\,,
            %p
              where the first inequality is an equality precisely if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              is even, while the second is an equality precisely if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              is odd. Hence this provides a candidate
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit+of+an+adjunction"} unit
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi η
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/counit+of+an+adjunction"} counit
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϵ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon
            %p
              Hence by
              %a{:href => "adjoint+functor#UniversalArrow"} this characterization
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functors"} adjoint functors
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  the adjunction
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⌊
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⌋
                        %mo ⊣
                        %mi odd
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lfloor -/2 \rfloor \dashv odd
                  is equivalent to the condition that
                %p
                  for every
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo ≤
                        %mn 2
                        %mi k
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \leq 2 k + 1
                  we have
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mn 2
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⌊
                          %mi n
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mn 2
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⌋
                          %mo +
                          %mn 1
                          %mo ≤
                          %mn 2
                          %mi k
                          %mo +
                          %mn 1
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2 \lfloor n/2 \rfloor + 1  \leq 2 k + 1
              %li
                %p
                  the adjunction
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi even
                        %mo ⊣
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⌊
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⌋
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} even \dashv \lfloor -/2 \rfloor
                  is equivalent to the condition that
                %p
                  for every
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn 2
                        %mi k
                        %mo ≤
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2k \leq n
                  we have
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mn 2
                          %mi k
                          %mo ≤
                          %mn 2
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⌊
                          %mi n
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mn 2
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⌋
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2k \leq 2 \lfloor n/2 \rfloor
            %p which is readily seen to be the case
          %p
            In the same vein there is an example for an adjunction of the form
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ◯
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mo □
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcirc \dashv \Box
          .num_example
            %h6#example_2 Example
            %p
              Consider the inclusion
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ι
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo ,
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ↪
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo ,
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \iota \colon (\mathbb{Z}, \lt) \hookrightarrow (\mathbb{R}, \lt)
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integers"} integers
              into the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+orders"} linear orders
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
              given by
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ceiling"} ceiling
              and a right adjoint given by
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/floor"} floor
            %p
              The composite
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ceiling
                    %mo ≔
                    %mi ι
                    %mi ceiling
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ceiling \coloneqq \iota ceiling
              is an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/idempotent+monad"} idempotent monad
              and the composite
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Floor
                    %mo ≔
                    %mi ι
                    %mi floor
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Floor \coloneqq \iota floor
              is an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/idempotent+comonad"} idempotent comonad
              on
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℝ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
              %em moment of integrality
              in an real number, but in opposite ways, each real number
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\in \mathbb{R}
              sits in between its floor and celling
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Floor
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi x
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi Ceiling
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    Floor(x) \leq x \leq Ceiling(x)
                    \\,.
            %p
              Indeed the moments form an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction"} adjunction
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ceiling
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mi Floor
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    Ceiling \dashv Floor
                    \\,.
          %h3#Werden
            Werden : Nichts
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊣
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \dashv
            Sein
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
            a
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} (∞,1)-topos
            consider the “initial topos”, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+category"} terminal category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo *
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi Sh
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ∅
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast \simeq Sh(\emptyset)
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+sheaves"} category of sheaves
            on the empty site).
          %p
            There is then an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+triple"} adjoint triple
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                  %mover
                    %mover
                      %munder
                        %mo ⟵
                        %mrow
                          %mo ⊢
                          %mo *
                      %mover
                        %mo ⟶
                        %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mo ⟵
                      %mrow
                        %mo ⊢
                        %mo ∅
                  %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbf{H}
                  \\stackrel
                  {\overset{\vdash \varnothing}{\longleftarrow}}
                  {\stackrel{\overset{}{\longrightarrow}}
                  {\underset{\vdash \ast}{\longleftarrow}}}
                  \\ast
          %p
            given by including the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/initial+object"} initial object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∅
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varnothing
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
            into
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
          %p
            In the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
            this corresponds to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+pair"} adjoint pair
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modalities"} modalities
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∅
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\varnothing \dashv \ast
          %p
            which are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constant+functor"} constant
            on the
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/initial+object"} initial object
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal object
          %p
            The induced
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit+of+an+adjunction"} unit of an adjunction
            transformation is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo ∅
                          %mo ⟶
                          %mi X
                          %mo ⟶
                          %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \\varnothing \longrightarrow X \longrightarrow \ast
                  }
          %p
            hence the unique factorization of the unique function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∅
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varnothing \longrightarrow \ast
            through any other
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"} type
          %p
            Looking through (
            %a{:href => "#Hegel1812"}> Hegel 1812, vol 1, book 1, section 1, chapter 1
            ) one might call
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ∅
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \emptyset
            “nothing”, call
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
            “being” and then call this unity of opposites “becoming”. In particular in §174 of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Science+of+Logic"} Science of Logic
            it says
          %blockquote
            %p there is nothing which is not an intermediate state between being and nothing
          %p which seems to be well-captured by the above unity transformation.
          %h3#Mengen
            Quantity : discreteness
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊣
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \dashv
            continuity
          %p
            The adjoint modality in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+topos"} local topos
            is that given by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+modality"} flat modality
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊣
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \dashv
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sharp+modality"} sharp modality
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ♭
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mo ♯
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\flat \dashv \sharp
                  \\,.
          %p
            Capturing
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+objects"} discrete objects
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/codiscrete+objects"} codiscrete objects
          %p The corresponding unity transformation is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ♭
                  %mi X
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi X
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mo ♯
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\flat X \longrightarrow X \longrightarrow \sharp X
          %p
            According to (
            %a{:href => "#Lawvere94"}> Lawvere 94, p. 6
            ) this unity captures the duality that in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elements"} elements
            are distinct and yet indistinguishable, an apparent
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/paradox"} paradox
            that may be traced back to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Georg+Cantor"} Georg Cantor
          %p
            Looking through Hegel’s
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Science+of+Logic"} Science of Logic
            at
            %em
              %a{:href => "#Science+of+Logic#OnDiscretenessAndRepulsion"} On discreteness and repulsion
            one can see that matches with what Hegel calls
          %blockquote
            %p (par 398) Quantity is the unity of these moments of continuity and discreteness
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo ♭
                          %mi X
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⟶
                        %mtd
                          %mi X
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⟶
                        %mtd
                          %mo ♯
                          %mi X
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mfrac{:linethickness => "0"}
                            %mrow
                              %mi moment
                              %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                              %mi of
                            %mrow
                              %mi discreteness
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mfrac{:linethickness => "0"}
                            %mrow
                              %mi moment
                              %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                              %mi of
                            %mrow
                              %mi continuity
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \\flat X &amp;\longrightarrow&amp; X &amp;\longrightarrow&amp; \sharp X
                  \\\
                  {moment\;of \atop discreteness} &amp;&amp; &amp;&amp; {moment\;of \atop continuity}
                  }
          %h3#ContinuumRepulsionCohesion
            Continuum : repulsion
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊣
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \dashv
            cohesion
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
            a
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+topos"} cohesive topos
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} cohesive (∞,1)-topos
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/shape+modality"} shape modality
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊣
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \dashv
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+modality"} flat modality
            constitute an adjoint cylinder
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ʃ
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mo ♭
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \&amp;#643; \dashv \flat
                  \\,.
          %p
            The corresponding unity-transformation is the
            %a{:href => "cohesive%20topos#CanonicalComparison"} points-to-pieces transform
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo ♭
                          %mi X
                          %mo ⟶
                          %mi X
                          %mo ⟶
                          %mi ʃ
                          %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \\flat X \longrightarrow X \longrightarrow &amp;#643; X
                  }
          %p
            Looking through (
            %a{:href => "#Hegel1812"}> Hegel 1812, vol 1, book 1, section 2, chapter 1
            ) one might call
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ♭
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \flat
            “repulsion”, call
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ʃ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} &amp;#643;
            “attraction”/“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesion"}> cohesion
            ” and then call this unity of opposites “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuum"}> continuum
            ”. Indeed, by the discussion at
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+topos"} cohesive topos
          %h3#ContinuumRepulsionCohesion
            Infinitesimal Continuum : infin. repulsion
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊣
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \dashv
            infinit. cohesion
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{H}
            equipped moreover with
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohesion"} differential cohesion
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+object"} infinitesimal
            version of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/shape+modality"} shape modality
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊣
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \dashv
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+modality"} flat modality
            namely the adjoint modality
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+shape+modality"} infinitesimal shape modality
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊣
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \dashv
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+flat+modality"} infinitesimal flat modality
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℑ
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mi &amp;
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Im \dashv \&amp;
                  \\,.
          %p The corresponding unity-transformation is the
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi &amp;
                          %mi X
                          %mo ⟶
                          %mi X
                          %mo ⟶
                          %mi ℑ
                          %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \\&amp; X \longrightarrow X \longrightarrow \Im X
                  }
          %p
            maps from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coefficients"} coefficients
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/crystalline+cohomology"} crystalline cohomology
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Rham+space"} de Rham space
            of types
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p In view of the above the unity exhibited here is clearly to be called the “infinitesimal continuum”.
          %h3#cohesive_sets Cohesive sets
          %p
            The combination of the above two examples of
            %a{:href => "#ContinuumRepulsionCohesion"} Continuum
            and
            %a{:href => "#Mengen"} Quantity
            is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+triple"} adjoint triple
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modalities"} modalities
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ʃ
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ♭
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ♯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \&amp;#643; \;\dashv\; \flat \;\dashv\; \sharp
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/shape+modality"} shape modality
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊣
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \dashv
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+modality"} flat modality
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊣
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \dashv
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sharp+modality"} sharp modality
          %p
            characteristic of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+topos"} cohesive topos
          %h3#skeleta_and_coskeleta Skeleta and Co-Skeleta
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+skeleton"} simplicial skeleton
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊣
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \dashv
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+coskeleton"} simplicial coskeleton
          %h3#FormalCompletionAndTorsionApproximation
            Formal completion
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊣
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \dashv
            Torsion approximation
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+ring"} commutative ring
            or more generally an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E-%E2%88%9E+ring"} E-∞ ring
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔞
                  %mo ⊂
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 0
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{a}\subset \pi_0 A
            a suitable ideal, then
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{a}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adic+completion"} adic completion
            and
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{a}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/torsion+approximation"} torsion approximation
            form an adjoint modality on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mi MMod
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A MMod
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} stable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category+of+%E2%88%9E-modules"} (∞,1)-category of ∞-modules
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %msub
                    %mi Mod
                    %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A Mod_\infty
            over
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          %p
            (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{a}
            \-adic completion)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊣
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \dashv
            (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{a}
            \-torsion approximation)
          #CompletionTorsionAdjointModalityForModuleSpectra.num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              be an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E-%E2%88%9E+ring"} E-∞ ring
              and let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔞
                    %mo ⊂
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 0
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{a} \subset \pi_0 A
              be a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generators+and+relations"} finitely generated
              ideal in its underlying
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+ring"} commutative ring
            %p
              Then there is an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+triple"} adjoint triple
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-functors"} adjoint (∞,1)-functors
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %munderover
                              %mo ≃
                              %mrow
                                %mi A
                                %msubsup
                                  %mi Mod
                                  %mrow
                                    %mi 𝔞
                                    %mi comp
                                  %mi op
                              %mrow
                                %mi A
                                %msubsup
                                  %mi Mod
                                  %mrow
                                    %mi 𝔞
                                    %mi tors
                                  %mi op
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mover
                                %munder
                                  %mo ⟵
                                  %mrow
                                    %msub
                                      %mo ♭
                                      %mi 𝔞
                                %mo ↪
                              %mover
                                %mo ⟵
                                %mrow
                                  %msub
                                    %mi Π
                                    %mi 𝔞
                          %mtd
                            %mi A
                            %msup
                              %mi Mod
                              %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    \\underoverset{
                    A Mod_{\mathfrak{a}comp}^{op}}
                    {A Mod_{\mathfrak{a}tors}^{op}}
                    {\simeq}
                    \&amp;\stackrel{\overset{\Pi_{\mathfrak{a}}}{\longleftarrow}}{\stackrel{\hookrightarrow}{\underset{\flat_{\mathfrak{a}}}{\longleftarrow}}}&amp;
                    A Mod^{op}
                    }
            %p where
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                        %mi Mod
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A Mod
                  is the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} stable
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category+of+modules"} (∞,1)-category of modules
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-modules"} ∞-modules
                  over
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi A
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                        %msub
                          %mi Mod
                          %mrow
                            %mi 𝔞
                            %mi tor
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A Mod_{\mathfrak{a}tor}
                  and
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                        %msub
                          %mi Mod
                          %mrow
                            %mi 𝔞
                            %mi comp
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A Mod_{\mathfrak{a} comp}
                  are the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+sub-%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} full sub-(∞,1)-categories
                  of
                  = succeed "-" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi 𝔞
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{a}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/torsion+approximation"} torsion
                  and of
                  = succeed "-" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi 𝔞
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{a}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/completion+of+a+module"} complete
                  = succeed "-" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi A
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-modules"} ∞-modules
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %msup
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mi op
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-)^{op}
                  denotes the
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opposite+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} opposite (∞,1)-category
              %li
                %p
                  the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} equivalence of (∞,1)-categories
                  on the left is induced by the restriction of
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi Π
                          %mi 𝔞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_{\mathfrak{a}}
                  and
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mo ♭
                            %mi 𝔞
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \flat_{\mathfrak{a}}
          .proof
            %h6#proof_2 Proof
            %p
              This is effectively the content of (
              %a{:href => "#LurieProper"}> Lurie “Proper morphisms”, section 4
              ):
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  the existence of
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi Π
                          %mi 𝔞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_{\mathfrak{a}}
                  is corollary 4.1.16 and remark 4.1.17
              %li
                %p
                  the existence of
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mo ♭
                          %mi 𝔞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \flat_{\mathfrak{a}}
                  is lemma 4.2.2 there;
              %li
                %p
                  the equivalence of sub-
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn ∞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
                  \-categories is proposition 4.2.5 there.
          %p
            See at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fracture+theorem"} fracture theorem
            for more.
          %h3#fermions_and_supergeometry Fermions and supergeometry
          %p
            On
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+smooth+infinity-groupoids"} super smooth infinity-groupoids
            there is an adjoint modality deriving from the
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "super+algebra#AdjointsToInclusionOfPlainAlgebra"} adjoint triple relating plain algebra and superalgebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoint"} right adjoint
            deserves to be called the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bosonic+modality"} bosonic modality
            (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/body"}> body
            ”), hence its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
            the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fermionic+modality"} fermionic modality
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergeometry"} supergeometry
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fermions"} fermions
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pauli+exclusion+principle"} Pauli exclusion principle
            %a{:href => "Science+of+Logic#290"} PN§290
            this unity of opposites might hence be called “asunderness”.
          %h3#totally_distributive_categories Totally distributive categories
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒦
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{K}
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/totally+distributive+category"} totally distributive category
            it induces on its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+presheaves"} category of presheaves
            an adjoint modality whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoint"} right adjoint
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+embedding"} Yoneda embedding
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            postcomposed with its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %h3#recollements Recollements
          %p
            See at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/recollement"} recollement
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Aufhebung"} Aufhebung
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+type+theory"} modal type theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+logic"} adjoint logic
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+being"} category of being
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Galois+connection"} Galois connection
          %h2#references References
          %h3#traditional Traditional
          %p
            The concept of
            %em dialectical reasoning
            is usually attributed to
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Plato"}> Plato
              , second part of the
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Parmenides+dialogue"} Parmenides dialogue
              \.
          %p See
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Georg+Hegel"}> Georg Hegel
              ,
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lectures+on+the+History+of+Philosophy"} Lectures on the History of Philosophy
                –
                %a{:href => "Lectures+on+the+History+of+Philosophy#ParmenidesDialogue"} Plato – Dialectic – Parmenides dialogue
          %p
            Hegel in his
            %em
              %a{:href => "https://www.marxists.org/reference/archive/hegel/works/hp/hpeleatics.htm"} History of Philosophy
            writes that dialectic begins with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Zeno"} Zeno
            (one of the characters in that dialogue).
          %p This is much amplified and expanded in
          %ul
            %li#Hegel1812
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Georg+Hegel"}> Georg Hegel
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Science+of+Logic"} Science of Logic
          %p
            The origins of its proposed formalization in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            are recalled in
          %ul
            %li#Lambek82
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Joachim+Lambek"}> Joachim Lambek
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em The Influence of Heraclitus on Modern Mathematics
              = succeed "," do
                %em Scientific Philosophy Today: Essays in Honor of Mario Bunge
          %p See also
          %ul
            %li#Wandschneider99
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dieter+Wandschneider"}> Dieter Wandschneider
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Dialektik als Letztbegründung der Logik
                %em Festschrift für Sok-Zin Lim
                Seoul 1999, 255–278 (
                %a{:href => "http://www.philosophie.rwth-aachen.de/global/show_document.asp?id=aaaaaaaaaabpltw"}> pdf
                )
            %li#Wikipedia
              %p
                Wikipedia,
                %em
                  %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hegelian_dialectic"} Hegelian dialectic
          %h3#in_terms_of_adjoint_triples_of_coreflections_and_localizations In terms of adjoint triples of (co-)reflections and localizations
          %p
            Conceived of in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+triples"} adjoint triples
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflective+subcategory"} (co-)reflections
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localization+of+a+category"} localizations
            the concept appears in
          %ul
            %li#Lawvere91
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"}> William Lawvere
                ,
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Some+Thoughts+on+the+Future+of+Category+Theory"} Some Thoughts on the Future of Category Theory
                in A. Carboni, M. Pedicchio, G. Rosolini,
                %em Category Theory
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Como"} Proceedings of the International Conference held in Como
            %li#Lawvere94
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"}> William Lawvere
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cohesive+Toposes+and+Cantor%27s+%22lauter+Einsen%22"} Cohesive Toposes and Cantor's "lauter Einsen"
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/LawvereCohesiveToposes.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
            %li#Lawvere94b
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"}> William Lawvere
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tools+for+the+advancement+of+objective+logic"}> Tools for the advancement of objective logic
                    \: closed categories and toposes
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gonzalo+Reyes"} Gonzalo Reyes
                (Eds.),
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The Logical Foundations of Cognition
              %p pages 43-56, 1994.
            %li#Lawvere96
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"}> William Lawvere
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Unity+and+Identity+of+Opposites+in+Calculus+and+Physics"} Unity and Identity of Opposites in Calculus and Physics
            %li#Lawvere00
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/F.+W.+Lawvere"}> F. W. Lawvere
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Adjoint Cylinders
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.mta.ca/~cat-dist/archive/2000/00-11"} link
            %li#LawvereRosebrugh03
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"}> William Lawvere
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robert+Rosebrugh"} Robert Rosebrugh
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sets+for+Mathematics"} Sets for Mathematics
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9780511755460"} doi:10.1017/CBO9780511755460
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.mta.ca/~rrosebru/setsformath/"} book homepage
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://books.google.de/books?id=h3_7aZz9ZMoC&pg=PP1&dq=sets+for+mathematics"} GoogleBooks
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://patryshev.com/books/Sets%20for%20Mathematics.pdf"} pdf
          %h3#in_terms_of_adjoint_pairs_of_modal_operators In terms of adjoint pairs of modal operators
          %p
            In terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+pairs"} adjoint pairs
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+operators"} modal operators
            and hence of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Galois+connections"} Galois connections
          %ul
            %li#ReyesZolfaghari91
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gonzalo+Reyes"}> Gonzalo Reyes
                , H. Zolfaghari,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Topos-theoretic approaches to modality
            %li#Reyes91
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gonzalo+Reyes"}> Gonzalo Reyes
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A topos-theoretic approach to reference and modality
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://projecteuclid.org/euclid.ndjfl/1093635834"} Euclid
          %p with further developments in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                M. Sadrzadeh, R. Dyckho,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Positive logic with adjoint modalities: Proof theory, semantics and reasoning about information
                %em Proceedings of the 25th Conference on Mathematical Foundations of Programming Semantics
                (MFPS 2009).
            %li#Hermida10
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Claudio+Hermida"}> Claudio Hermida
                , section 3.3. of
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A categorical outlook on relational modalities and simulations
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://maggie.cs.queensu.ca/chermida/papers/sat-sim-IandC.pdf"} pdf
            %li#DJK14
              %p
                Wojciech Dzik, Jouni Järvinen, Michiro Kondo,
                %em Characterising intermediate tense logics in terms of Galois connections
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1401.7646"}> arXiv:1401.7646
                )
          %p
            Formalization specifically in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+type+theory"} modal type theory
            is in
          %ul
            %li#LicataShulman
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dan+Licata"}> Dan Licata
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Shulman"} Mike Shulman
              %em Adjoint logic with a 2-category of modes
              (
              %a{:href => "http://dlicata.web.wesleyan.edu/pubs/ls15adjoint/ls15adjoint.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p For an overview of the role of adjunctions in modal logic see:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mat%C3%ADas+Menni"}> M. Menni
              , C. Smith,
              %em Modes of Adjointness
              , J. Philos. Logic
              %strong 43
              no.3-4 (2014) pp.365-391.
          %h3#examples_2 Examples
          %ul
            %li#LurieProper
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"}> Jacob Lurie
              , section 4 of
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Proper+Morphisms%2C+Completions%2C+and+the+Grothendieck+Existence+Theorem"} Proper Morphisms, Completions, and the Grothendieck Existence Theorem
